"""
What this project serves for a requested locale tag. `served.canonicalCode` differing from `requested` means the request fell back; `served` being null means the project serves nothing for it and every field comes back in its authored source value.
"""
type LocaleResolution {
  """
  The tag as the read path sees it: canonicalised ("en-gb" becomes "en-GB") when it is valid BCP 47, verbatim when it is not. Compare against `served.canonicalCode` to tell an exact hit from a fallback. Comparing against the tag you sent gives the wrong answer for any spelling canonicalisation changes, and comparing against `served.code` gives the wrong answer when the project stores its locale non-canonically.
  """
  requested: String!

  """
  The locale this project resolves the request to: the first rung of the fallback chain that the project actually configures and still serves. Null when it serves none of them. This is the locale to put in `<html lang>` and to read `isRtl` from. It is not a per-field guarantee: a field carrying no value in this locale still falls through the rest of the chain to its authored default.
  """
  served: ProjectLocale
}

